Minimal arm64 FDT/GICv3/PL011 payload
=====================================

guest.elf is a freestanding, fixed-address AArch64 executable for manually
exercising the first vmd arm64 backend.  vmd loads it at 0x40000000, builds a
minimal FDT in the last 16KB page of guest RAM, and enters the payload with the
FDT guest-physical address in x2.  The payload resolves the polling PL011 and
the arm,gic-v3 node from that table, installs an EL1 vector table, configures
SPI 33, and software-pends it through GICD_ISPENDR.  Its handler acknowledges
the INTID through ICC_IAR1_EL1 and writes ICC_EOIR1_EL1 before printing the
success line.  This checks FDT handoff, 8/32/64-bit GIC MMIO, trapped ICC
system registers, VMM_IOC_INTR delivery, architectural IRQ entry, and EOI.

Build vmd and the payload:

	cd /usr/src/usr.sbin/vmd
	make
	cd /usr/src/regress/usr.sbin/vmd/arm64
	make guest.elf

Make sure the installed vmd service is stopped, then run the newly built
daemon in one terminal:

	cd /usr/src/usr.sbin/vmd
	doas ./vmd -d -f /dev/null

Start and attach to the toy guest from another terminal:

	doas vmctl start -c -m 64M \
	    -b /usr/src/regress/usr.sbin/vmd/arm64/guest.elf arm64-fdt

The console should print:

	arm64 vmd FDT + GICv3 SPI interrupt works

The payload then issues HVC and remains parked without consuming a host CPU.
Detach with the vmctl console escape and stop it when finished:

	doas vmctl stop -f arm64-fdt

The automated form builds the same ELF and manages a private vmd instance:

	cd /usr/src/regress/usr.sbin/vmd/arm64
	doas make VMD=/usr/src/usr.sbin/vmd/vmd regress

Full disk installation (manual integration test)
===============================================

The PCI platform can install and boot an ordinary arm64 system using the
existing virtio block and network drivers.  Use a NEW disposable image:

	vmctl create -s 80G qcow2:/path/to/disk.qcow2
	doas vmctl start -c -m 1G -L -b /path/to/bsd.rd \
	    -d qcow2:/path/to/disk.qcow2 arm64-install

Use a matching bsd.rd and signed install sets, and select all the standard
sets.  The local interface (-L) supplies DHCP but does not provide Internet
forwarding or DNS service.  Configure external connectivity separately, or
attach a second FFS disk containing the sets and SHA256.sig under
/8.0/arm64 with another -d raw:/path/to/sets.img option.  In that example,
sd0 is the installation target and sd1 is the media; initialize only sd0.

After the installer finishes, export its completed /mnt/bsd to a file in
the outer VM.  A writable partition on the separate installation-media
disk can carry this file and /mnt/var/db/kernel.SHA256.  Unmount that
partition, halt the guest and wait for disk synchronization before stopping
the VM or mounting its media in the outer system.  Check the exported
kernel against the recorded checksum, then boot without the media disk:

	doas vmctl start -c -m 1G -L -b /path/to/bsd.installed \
	    -d qcow2:/path/to/disk.qcow2 arm64-installed

The current loader directly loads an ELF kernel; it does not run firmware
or a disk boot loader.  The FDT does not yet supply a boot-disk ID, so enter
sd0a at the root-device prompt and accept the default sd0b swap device.
Export /bsd again after changing the installed kernel.  Kernel symbols and
an initial random seed are not yet passed by this direct-boot path; the
corresponding boot warnings are separate from PCI/disk attachment.  Set the
guest clock after boot if network time is unavailable.

A successful test must reach the installed system, not just the ramdisk
shell: check the root mount, disk capacity and DUID, completion of every set,
the installed /bsd.rd checksum against the signed manifest, and SSH/network
traffic.  The 2026-09-30 snapshot passed these checks with an 80GB qcow2
disk, a GENERIC kernel, and host-only virtio networking.  Its 512MB installer
completed, including kernel relinking, but used guest swap during the long
relink; the installed-system boot used 1GB.  The outer VM remained at 6GB
and Linux host swap was unused.  Remember that a sparse 80GB image does not
reserve 80GB of space on its backing filesystem.
